  "user_handle": "Veritas",
  "body": "Vulnerability details\n\nFinding description and impact\n\nRoot Cause In \nAtomWallet.sol\n, the abstract account intercepts ERC-4337 validateUserOp requests through _validateSignature and subsequently calls extractValidUntilAndValidAfter to decode transaction expiration bounds from the first 24 bytes of the callData payload.\n\nTo achieve this, the contract attempts to slice the calldata into 12-byte chunks and dynamically decodes them using abi.decode:\n\nsolidity\nvalidUntil = abi.decode(callData[:12], (uint256));\nvalidAfter = abi.decode(callData[12:24], (uint256));\nThis is fundamentally invalid in Solidity. abi.decode strictly enforces EVM ABI encoding specifications. For a statically-sized type like uint256, the EVM expects exactly 32 bytes (1 padded word) of input data. Providing a 12-byte slice (callData[:12]) violates length bounds-checking.\n\nAs a result, abi.decode throws an out-of-bounds InsufficientDataBytes error and guarantees a revert on every execution attempt.\n\nImpact Because extractValidUntilAndValidAfter is inextricably linked to the core _validateSignature ERC-4337 flow, this single strict bounds violation causes every single validateUserOp sequence to crash.\n\nThe transaction EntryPoint evaluator will irrevocably reject all relayed/gasless operations. This permanently locks all users out of ERC-4337 execution abstractions globally across the protocol and completely bricks the AtomWallet smart wallet infrastructure.\n\nRecommended mitigation steps\nRemove the inflexible abi.decode engine in favor of explicit byte-casting logic. This correctly right-pads the 12-byte slice into a standard 32-byte uint256 capacity without triggering explicit bounds-checking validation:\n\ndiff\n-        validUntil = abi.decode(callData[:12], (uint256));\n-        validAfter = abi.decode(callData[12:24], (uint256));\n+        validUntil = uint256(uint96(bytes12(callData[:12])));\n+        validAfter = uint256(uint96(bytes12(callData[12:24])));",
  "proof_of_concept": "To empirically demonstrate that abi.decode explicitly reverts when receiving less than 32 bytes for a uint256, run the following Foundry test.\n\nSave it in the test/unit/ folder and run forge test --match-contract AtomWalletDecodeFail. The test succeeds, guaranteeing the EVM revert behavior:\n\nsolidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.21;\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\ncontract AtomWalletDecodeFail is Test {\n    \n    // Simulates exactly what extractValidUntilAndValidAfter does\n    function extractValidUntilAndValidAfter(bytes calldata callData) \n        internal \n        pure \n        returns (uint256 validUntil, uint256 validAfter) \n    {\n        // abi.decode strictly checks if the input is >= 32 bytes.\n        // The slice is 12 bytes long. This is guaranteed to revert.\n        validUntil = abi.decode(callData[:12], (uint256));\n        validAfter = abi.decode(callData[12:24], (uint256));\n        \n        return (validUntil, validAfter);\n    }\n    // External wrapper to pass memory payload as calldata, identical to entryPoint flow\n    function _validateSignatureMock(bytes calldata callData) external pure {\n        extractValidUntilAndValidAfter(callData);\n    }\n    function test_validateUserOp_AlwaysRevertsOnDecode() public {\n        // Create an empty payload >= 24 bytes, mirroring ERC-4337 validation payloads\n        bytes memory mockPayload = new bytes(64);\n        \n        // The ERC-4337 _validateSignature hook processes the payload. It will reliably REVERT.\n        vm.expectRevert(); \n        this._validateSignatureMock(mockPayload);\n    }\n}",
